<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">League of God's House</h1><p class="st-meta">1367 CE – 1799 CE · capital Chur</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">One of the Three Leagues of the Grisons, formed in 1367 by the bishop of Chur's subjects to stop him handing their land to the Habsburgs.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1367 the cathedral chapter, the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chur">Chur</a> and the communes of the Engadine, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bregaglia">Bergell</a> and other valleys under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-chur">Prince-Bishopric of Chur</a> met to prevent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> Peter Gelyto from handing his temporal rule to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>. Their pact became the League of God's House (Gotteshausbund), named after the cathedral.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The league was a federation of self-governing communes, each with its own courts and assemblies, which sent envoys to a common diet. It allied with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/grey-league">Grey League</a> in 1406 and with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/league-of-the-ten-jurisdictions">League of the Ten Jurisdictions</a> in 1450, forming the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/three-leagues">Three Leagues</a>, an associate of the Swiss. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> divided its communes, and much of the Engadine turned Reformed.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The league remained part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/three-leagues">Three Leagues</a> until 1799, when French forces attached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graubunden">Grisons</a> to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/helvetic-republic">Helvetic Republic</a>. In 1803 the Grisons became a Swiss canton, and the old leagues' symbols survive in its coat of arms.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/league-of-gods-house.jpg" alt="League of God's House" width="320" height="322"><figcaption>Image: Unknown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CHE_Gotteshausbund_Flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1367 CE – 1799 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chur</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism, Reformed Protestantism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Romansh, German, Italian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Gotteshausbund, Lia da la Chadé</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1583&amp;focus=league-of-gods-house" aria-label="League of God's House on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4904 532 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where League of God's House lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5224 788L5224 788"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5224 788h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5224 788h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5224 788h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Chur. <a href="/atlas?year=1583&amp;focus=league-of-gods-house">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

